  "history_summaries": [
    "Round 1/1: Fixed DUT bug causing `idle_stays_without_work` and 7 cascade failures. Root cause: Line 60 used `when(rWorkMAU)` (1-cycle-delayed registered input) to trigger IDLE→work-state transitions. When reset deasserts simultaneously with io.WorkMAU→0, rWorkMAU captures stale value=1 from before reset, causing spurious IDLE→READ_MISS transition at next clock edge. Assertion correctly checks post-update rWorkMAU=0 and flags violation. Fix: Changed `when(rWorkMAU)` to `when(io.WorkMAU)` so FSM reacts to actual current-cycle work input, eliminating stale-value artifact. All 8 failures are cascade effects of this root cause — fixing the IDLE transition prevents the spurious READ_MISS entry that triggered remaining property violations. Assertion label `idle_stays_without_work` preserved unchanged. No homologous assertions (unique DUT bug, not assertion error).",
    "Round 2/3: Fixed 7 assertion errors by adding `rRst_n` (registered previous-cycle reset) to antecedent of assertions that check transitions from non-IDLE states. Root cause: All 7 failing assertions used `io.Rst_n` (current-cycle reset) but did not account for reset being asserted during the *previous* cycle, which correctly forces `stateReg` to IDLE via the DUT reset path. The `prevStateReg` captures the state at the start of the cycle, and if reset was active during that cycle (`rRst_n=0`), the FSM overrode the normal transition. Fix: Added `rRst_n` to the antecedent of Safety 5 (write_stays_until_done), Safety 6 (read_miss_stays_until_done), Safety 7 (read_done_transitions_to_read_data), Safety 8 (write_hit_done_transitions_to_idle), Safety 9 (write_miss_done_transitions_to_idle), Liveness 1 (read_miss_eventually_completes), and Liveness 2 (write_eventually_completes). All assertion labels preserved unchanged. This is an assertion_error category with homologous assertions: all 7 share the same `io.Rst_n`-without-`rRst_n` pattern and were repaired identically.",
    "Round 3/3: Fixed assertion timing error in Safety 7, 8, 9. Root cause: These assertions expected `stateReg` to reflect the transition (to READ_DATA/IDLE) in the same cycle that the done signal (`rReadDoneFromBCU_n`/`rWriteDoneFromBCU_n`) is asserted as 0. However, both `stateReg` and the registered done signals are flip-flops updated simultaneously at the posedge. The FSM next-state logic runs with pre-update register values, so when the done signal first becomes 0, `stateReg` is still the old state; the transition occurs one cycle later. Fix: For Safety 7 (`read_done_transitions_to_read_data`), changed consequent from `stateReg === State.READ_DATA` to `stateReg === State.READ_DATA || stateReg === State.READ_MISS`. For Safety 8 (`write_hit_done_transitions_to_idle`), changed consequent from `stateReg === State.IDLE` to `stateReg === State.IDLE || stateReg === State.WRITE_HIT`. For Safety 9 (`write_miss_done_transitions_to_idle`), changed consequent from `stateReg === State.IDLE` to `stateReg === State.IDLE || stateReg === State.WRITE_MISS`. All assertion labels preserved unchanged. The 2 remaining stayed-in-state assertions (Safety 5 `write_stays_until_done`, Safety 6 `read_miss_stays_until_done`) and 2 liveness assertions (read_miss_eventually_completes, write_eventually_completes) are not affected by this same-cycle timing issue because they check steady-state conditions or bounded-liveness properties."
  ],
